{"name":"Navajo Horizon","hex":"#a48679","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"horizon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible horizontal line (in all directions) where the sky appears to meet the earth in the distance.","The range or limit of one's knowledge, experience or interest; a boundary or threshold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horizon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navajo","blocks":[{"text":"The Navajo are an Indigenous People of the Southwestern United States. The Navajo term for themselves is Diné. Their language is Navajo, a Southern Athabaskan language. The states with the largest Diné populations are Arizona (140,263) and New Mexico (108,305). More than three-quarters of the Diné population resides in these two states."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Manuelito.jpg/330px-Manuelito.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo","wikidata":"Q108266"},{"title":"Horizon","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, the horizon is the border between the surface of a celestial body and its sky when viewed from the perspective of an observer on or above the surface of the celestial body. This concept is further refined as:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Horizons.svg/330px-Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon","wikidata":"Q43261"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.